0" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/1&#10;GENERATING RESEARCH QUESTIONS&#10;THROUGH PROBLEMATIZATION.&#10;year: 2011&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W2099043095&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="148" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 424&#10;trend_chars: 5975" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="920" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: Are there more color&#10;pigments to discover? Context: It is possible to create new&#10;pigments, but not new colors. The first synthetic pigment was&#10;made in the early 1700s. In 2009, chemist Mas Subramanian&#10;accidently created a new blue pigment called “YInMn Blue.”&#10;seed_paper: GENERATING RESEARCH QUESTIONS THROUGH&#10;PROBLEMATIZATION. # CITATION SUBGRAPH (0 refs, temporally&#10;ordered by year)&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: No graph-supported transition can&#10;be identified because the supplied citation subgraph contains&#10;no referenced papers. The seed material only frames the&#10;research question: whether additional color pigments can still&#10;be discovered, while distinguishing the creation of new&#10;pigments from the creation of new perceived colors. If the&#10;seed is treated as the starting node, its contribution is&#10;conceptual rather than methodological: it turns the broad&#10;question of pigment discovery into a problem about discovery&#10;pathways, novelty, and the limits of known pigment chemistry.&#10;Paper 1 to Paper 2: Not applicable from the provided graph.&#10;There are no explicit predecessor chains, parallel work&#10;patterns, or high-citation foundational papers in the&#10;subgraph, so no methodological evolution can be traced from&#10;one cited paper to another. No competing approaches can be&#10;compared, and no prevailing direction can be identified from&#10;structural citation signals. Key Turning Points: No turning&#10;points can be directly inferred from the citation graph&#10;because no paper-to-paper lineage is present. However, from&#10;the topic context, the major conceptual turning points in the&#10;broader research problem are: 1. The shift from natural&#10;pigments to synthetic pigments, beginning in the early 1700s,&#10;which established that pigment discovery could be driven by&#10;deliberate chemical synthesis rather than only by extraction&#10;from natural materials. 2. The continued relevance of&#10;accidental discovery in modern inorganic chemistry,&#10;exemplified by the 2009 discovery of YInMn Blue. This case&#10;shows that even after centuries of pigment development,&#10;unexpected stable or metastable inorganic phases can still&#10;produce technologically useful colors. 3. The implied future&#10;turning point: moving from serendipitous discovery toward&#10;predictive or design-driven pigment discovery. The central&#10;scientific challenge is whether new pigments can be found&#10;systematically by modeling composition, crystal structure,&#10;electronic structure, optical absorption, and perceived color&#10;together. Recurring Limitations: No recurring limitations can&#10;be observed across multiple papers in the supplied graph&#10;because the graph contains no cited papers. At the level of&#10;the research topic itself, several persistent scientific&#10;limitations are implicit: 1. Human color perception constrains&#10;“newness.” New pigments can provide n..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="920" width="560" height="1014" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="2034" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Inorganic color pigment discovery and materials&#10;chemistry, especially the search for new practical pigments&#10;through composition-structure-optical-property and human-&#10;color-perception modeling.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Current pigment discovery remains largely empirical&#10;and serendipitous because there is no robust predictive and&#10;inverse design framework that jointly links chemical&#10;composition, crystal structure, electronic structure, defect&#10;chemistry, particle morphology, synthesis feasibility,&#10;stability, toxicity, and perceived color to identify&#10;practically useful new pigments.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- chemical composition&#10;- crystal structure&#10;- electronic band structure&#10;- defect chemistry&#10;- particle morphology&#10;- spectral reflectance&#10;- optical absorption&#10;- perceived color&#10;- hue&#10;- saturation&#10;- brightness&#10;- color gamut&#10;- metastable phase&#10;- synthesis pathway&#10;- thermal stability&#10;- chemical stability&#10;- lightfastness&#10;- toxicity&#10;- earth abundance&#10;- scalability&#10;- known pigment families&#10;- high-throughput screening&#10;- inverse design loop&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- chemical composition constrains accessible crystal&#10;structures and defect states&#10;- crystal structure and defects determine electronic structure&#10;and optical absorption&#10;- optical absorption and particle morphology shape spectral&#10;reflectance&#10;- spectral reflectance maps to perceived color through human&#10;visual response&#10;- synthesis conditions determine whether a phase can be&#10;obtained and retained&#10;- stability, toxicity, and manufacturability filter candidate&#10;pigments&#10;- inverse design maps desired color and property targets to&#10;candidate compositions&#10;- autonomous discovery cycles connect prediction, synthesis,&#10;characterization, and validation&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- predicting perceived color directly from composition and&#10;structure is unreliable&#10;- the viable pigment subset is narrow within vast inorganic&#10;chemical space&#10;- metastable phases are difficult to predict, synthesize, and&#10;retain&#10;- simultaneous optimization of color strength, safety,&#10;durability, and cost is hard&#10;- spectral novelty may not translate into perceptually&#10;meaningful new color&#10;- closed-loop pigment discovery remains insufficiently mature&#10;- exploration is concentrated in familiar oxide, sulfide, and&#10;silicate families&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- novel spectral response versus limited human color&#10;categories&#10;- vivid color versus chemical and thermal stability&#10;- new composition exploration versus safety and environmental&#10;constraints&#10;- serendipitous discovery versus systematic predictive design&#10;- metastable functionality versus practical synthesizability&#10;- additional functions such as infrared reflectance versus&#10;visual color performance&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- predictive composition-to-color capability&#10;- inverse design from desired perceptual and property targets&#10;- multi-objective optimization of color, stability, safety,&#10;abundance, and scalability&#10;- ability to evaluate metastability and synthesis feasibility&#10;- integration of human color perception and spectral novelty&#10;metrics&#10;- support for high-throughput or autonomous experimental&#10;validation&#10;- capacity to explore beyond established pigment families&#10;while retaining practicality&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- physics-informed predictive modeling&#10;- generative inverse design&#10;- surrogate modeling of electronic and optical properties&#10;- human-vision-aware color mapping&#10;- multi-objective optimization&#10;- active learning and closed-loop autonomous experimentation&#10;- metastability and synthesis-feasibility prediction&#10;- defect and crystal-structure engineering&#10;- spectral novelty and gamut analysis&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- computational materials discovery workflows for inorganic&#10;compounds&#10;- electronic-structure and band-gap prediction methods&#10;- color science models of human perception and gamut&#10;- autonomous laboratory loops used for materials synthesis&#10;- high-throughput screening of stable and metastable phases&#10;- defect-engineering approaches in optical materials&#10;- multi-objective optimization in safe and durable materials&#10;design&#10;- exploration of oxide, sulfide, silicate, and related&#10;inorganic families for unusual optical responses&#10;- functional color materials with coupled thermal or&#10;environmental behavior" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="2034" width="560" height="2238" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: compressed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;compression: deterministic flash&#10;radius plan; skipped LLM gate" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="4372" width="260" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 1&#10;num_cross_d